A||||9||REDHOTT88||Plenum replacement..tough job?||||5||
Z||000000||REDHOTT88||12-28-2001||05:39 PM||||I have two engines for my Fiero, (2.8 v6) the one IN the car has a peeling plenum. The other engine has NICE shiny plenum, but bad bearings.<P>I want to swap them. I'm no mechanic, but I can handle most tasks. Any suggestions from someone whos done this? Can I get replacement gasket easy at parts store? Any thing to watch out for? Or replace while doing?<BR><P>------------------<BR>                                                                                                         <BR>               <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/FIREdevil.gif">     <FONT COLOR="ffoooo"> <B>1988 Formula 2.8L V6</B> 5 speed Getrag </FONT><BR>            <I><FONT SIZE="1">(Runs smooth, shifts crisp, goes fast, red and shiny all over!)</FONT></I>||||reg||5
Z||000001||avengador1||12-28-2001||06:27 PM||||Just make sure that you reconnect "ALL" the hoses or you will have a high idle problem caused by a vaccuum leak, and your manifolds will glow cherry red. Other than that it's a simple bolt in. Use some gasket sealer on the gaskets to make sure they seal well. If you can't find gaskets, the Fiero store has them. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfiero||12-28-2001||06:33 PM||||Swapping plenums is pretty easy and straightforward.  You will need a few tools.....a ratchet, a 12 point 10mm socket, a T-40 Torx bit, a 12 point 9mm socket, 7mm socket, pliers, common screwdriver and 100 wrench.<P>First loosen/remove the chrome bolts.  Almost all of tehse are the 12 point 10mm, but there is a 12 point 9mm bolts holding the coolant tubes to the throttle body.<P>Now remove the air intake hose and with the T-40 Torx bit remove the 2 bolts that hold the throttle body to the plenum.<P>With the 10mm wrench remove the 2 nuts that attach the EGR tube to the EGR valve riser.<P>Remove (with the 7mm socket) the MAP sensor....you may find it easier to remove the entire bracket (there is a 10mm bolt underneath).<P>With the pliers remove the clip that holds the big hose to teh side of the plenum (passenger side) and pop off this hose.<P>There are a couple of vacuum lines that attach to the underside and sides of the plenum......carefully pop these off.<P>That's pretty much it......carefully lift up on the plenum (making sure everything is loose) and it should come right off.  Be careful with teh EGR tube as you snake it past the TB coolant tubes and you'll be OK.<P>You can re-use the gaskets as long as you don't break them during removal.<P>I like this method because you don't need to remove the throttle body.<P>I would carefully inspect the EGR tube for any cracks.  What I do is remove it completely and clean up one end.  Plug one end (presumably the dirtier side  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ) to creat a vacuum and blow into the "clean" end.  If there are any cracks it will leak.  Replace the tube if there are any cracks!<P>The other thing you can do is clean up your throttle body.....with the plenum off you can easily access the inside of the TB and get all of the gunk off of it.<P><BR>That's about it.....good luck. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||12-28-2001||07:08 PM||||be sure to soak them the nite before with some pen oil. A few of the torx screws broke off removing mine (just like the #%@* exhaust manifolds)||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfiero||12-28-2001||07:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>be sure to soak them the nite before with some pen oil. A few of the torx screws broke off removing mine (just like the #%@* exhaust manifolds)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That's what you get for living in the rust belt!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I have never had any problems with rusted/frozen bolts.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||REDHOTT88||12-28-2001||10:06 PM||||Hey thanks a lot everybody...    <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/thumbs.gif"> <P>That was what I needed.. I appreciate it.<P>    <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/aha.gif">  MR FIERO I'm going to "print out" those instuctions!! It'll be like having you standing over me. Ha Ha<BR><P>------------------<BR>                                                                                                         <BR>               <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/FIREdevil.gif">     <FONT COLOR="ffoooo"> <B>1988 Formula 2.8L V6</B> 5 speed Getrag </FONT><BR>            <I><FONT SIZE="1">(Runs smooth, shifts crisp, goes fast, red and shiny all over!)</FONT></I>||||reg||14
Z||000006||mrfiero||12-28-2001||10:12 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by REDHOTT88:<BR><B>     <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/aha.gif">   MR FIERO I'm going to "print out" those instuctions!! It'll be like having you standing over me. Ha Ha<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I was relying on my memory, so hopefully I didn't forget anything. <P>Good luck......it really isn't a hard thing to do.  You should be able to get it off within an hours time if you don't hit any snags (like those frozen bolts rogergarrison was talking about).<P>Just make sure you reattach the vacuum lines or your idle will be high!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||rogergarrison||12-29-2001||06:59 AM||||Hey, just a thought, but why not pass on the risk and just repaint the one on the car already? You dont even have to spray it. Eastwood has brush on heat proof paints, dries like powder coating, no brush marks.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 12-29-2001).]||||reg||3
